{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on declared-acts design head 0888322c73321e7ae039da031c56ce530a4b510e, request #10286 (git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a2cbd459e96c2ddb55c84f34eddb76ea63d0cfca). Both artifacts were inspected. This is a documentation review; it neither adopts the proposed design nor commissions implementation.\n\n1. P1 — Bind a signed act to its intended semantics across activation. notes/2026-10-02-declared-acts.md@0888322c:323 deliberately omits an expected declaration binding and judges an old signature under the new declaration. The claim that same-shape semantic changes are rare does not make them safe: change a thread-targeted act from [version] to [version, land], keeping the same body fields, roles and target. A previously signed request to stage a version can then start a landing after activation, without its signer agreeing to that action. Changes to fixed scopes and hold settings give further same-shape examples. Specify a signed semantic binding (whole-document or a precisely defined per-kind identity), a stale-binding refusal, and idempotent retry behavior for an already accepted act. State what happens to existing delegations and prepared checker jobs when their named kind changes meaning. Require acceptance cases for a same-shape change, an unrelated declaration update and an exact accepted retry. The cited dap source at 9d738e2e explicitly fences stale expected bindings for this reason; no arbitrary code is needed to reproduce this problem.\n\n2. P2 — Make the jam's thread and scope restrictions enforceable. Section 7's declarations select only a target shape and step. They do not restrict change-key to the song.json thread, pass-solo to a solo thread, or take-solo to a thread with solo settings. Section 1 instead fixes hold settings when any thread opens. Concrete case: release a take-part thread (14,400-second lease), then address take-solo to that unheld thread. The inherited take primitive accepts a caller-supplied/fixed new scope and retains the thread's settings; the proposed design supplies no predicate that rejects this cross-use. It can therefore produce a solo with the part's four-hour lease instead of 64 seconds. Likewise a leader holding a part can call change-key on it and land a part-file diff while the report claims only song.json can change. Add explicit thread applicability/scope predicates, using existing refuse inputs if sufficient, or define a small typed thread contract. Include the necessary rules in the worked example and tests for cross-thread use, reservation bypass and conflicting hold modes. Wording in a label or refusal is not enforcement.\n\n3. P2 — Specify an exact legacy vocabulary for v1 replay. Sections 3.1, 5.3 and 6 make the section-6 declarations the fixed meaning of every old v1 policy, but the displayed claim declaration omits purpose and section 3.4(a) moves config-recovery to recover. Existing schema.ts@df22d771:221-239 accepts purpose=config-recovery on claim; admission.ts:608-660 gives it distinct admin-only, policy-bypassing semantics. Rechecking such an old signed claim with the displayed declaration rejects its body or loses its recovery meaning. A wiped spike does not establish that no retained clone or test log contains this valid history. Separate the immutable legacy schema/semantics from new v2 authoring, describe their version identity and the transition of existing recovery threads, and require a fresh-clone replay fixture containing the whole old recovery sequence. Preserve the unchanged-v1 guarantee explicitly rather than relying on absence of production examples.\n\n4. P2 — Close the replay proof, not only the effect fold. Section 4:401-414 promises every admission decision from a fold of holder, lease generation, generation and reservation plus receipt effects. The listed primitives also depend on scope, review/check obligations and evidence, recomputation, prepared integrations/configuration, landing state, and Git reachability/diffs. Current LaneEffect lacks most of those facts; current ReplayContext is an evaluator-call context, not a complete witness of admission. Reading an accepted receipt's effect does not independently establish that the effect's guard held. Add a guard-to-retained-input/rederived-state table covering every primitive, specify how expected transitions are derived and compared with receipts, and make missing required material a named failure or explicit proof limit. Bound the fold and retained contexts. Include adversarial signed-log fixtures for a land with an unmet obligation, an out-of-scope version, an invalid reservation transition and forged receipt effects. Stage 6 may remain separately requested, but a later stage cannot by itself substitute for this design. Distinguish published decisions from unrecorded admission refusals and from room-clock truth.\n\n5. P2 — Apply the current operator correction. Section 7.3:843-845, stage 7:924 and the closing prior-art paragraph still assign self-hostability judgment to Hugh. Effective #10289 (git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b2cdc44a4fd2967f4ecd6a366dfa67df36f513b8) expressly assigns it to builder. Update those passages and their provenance. Keep the separately requested gate-1 recommendation for Hugh distinct from builder's self-hostability judgment.\n\nAlso finish the supersession inventory required by #10286: section 8.1 lists several rules/types, but it does not provide the requested complete plan-section, protocol-rule and contract-type inventory. At least R-LANE-2's declared compatibility gap and R-LOG-15's proposed verification-contract replacement need an explicit disposition. Distinguish a proposal to amend, an already superseded assumption and a retained rule. Clarify the jam role examples, which omit admin while the platform floor says admin may sign every declared act; choose implicit inclusion or a required explicit entry consistently. The statement that a check can compare the signed key field with song.json also needs its input path: current CheckJob carries Git inputs, not the proposing act body.\n\nArchitecture: the proposed direction correctly keeps signature, authority, persistence and effect execution in the platform while declarations supply application vocabulary as validated data. It remains Artroom design as ordinary Git content coordinated through Workroom; none of Gitseq's kernel/nexus/host/application-layer contracts is changed by this notes-only head. The current proposal still needs the semantic-binding, thread applicability and complete replay boundaries above to make application extensibility safe. A fixed primitive set is a reasonable starting point if its limits and extension process remain explicit; the full application scope must not be silently reduced to satisfy gate dates.\n\nSecurity: signature attribution alone does not preserve the signer's intended operation after a declaration changes. Admin authorization of a configuration update is not authorization to reinterpret another member's pending signature. Keep configuration recovery independent of application declarations, retain historical authority and semantics, and independently validate state transitions. The note's explicit no-build/no-adoption wording is appropriate. The effective planner request #10286, builder promise #10288 and the full request trailer bind this documentation delivery; its standing operator basis includes both #10285 and the correction #10289. Approval of a repaired note must still leave Hugh's marked decisions and later implementation requests explicit.\n\nSimplification: one policy document and one ordered activation path avoid competing catalogs; a closed primitive algebra avoids embedded application code. Retain those choices. Prefer a precise binding rule and a small applicability rule over special cases for the jam. Consolidate compatibility and replay obligations into tables tied to acceptance cases, rather than claiming them indirectly through repeated descriptions of current code.\n\nValidation: read all 1,018 lines and the index delta in clean disposable clone /tmp/artroom-checker-declared-acts-v1; inspected both exact artifacts and their direct builder promise, request, operator correction and commit trailer. Checked the relevant current schema/admission, policy inputs, checker job, lane effects and verifier limitations. Read the cited Gitseq record/schema-fold sources at 02090dc2d and dap's stale-binding semantics at 9d738e2e. git diff --check passes and the checkout is clean; only the note and plans index differ from df22d771. No runtime implementation or guard is added by this head, so no mutation result or freshly run root gate is claimed here. Builder's reported gates are attribution, not my independent evidence. No source edit, deployment or provider action occurred.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9529fab2e848f99ccf9812d8cef9f3c085b265a1","binding":"assigned","head":"0888322c73321e7ae039da031c56ce530a4b510e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:74d7b789bc92cdff9f2e0e025bc0f03a8b1b6926\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e635e71eca17ba273ad50729fd39b4380e887485\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:95e84afef5a456c833de85de0eb18ada9f0c9fd0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}